00:03:50.055 --> 00:03:51.143
Main systems.

67
00:03:51.423 --> 00:03:52.533
Digital ID.

68
00:03:53.883 --> 00:03:55.227
Here is a quick table:

69
00:03:55.507 --> 00:03:57.021
System, and What it does.

70
00:03:57.301 --> 00:03:58.517
Enrolment client:

71
00:03:58.797 --> 00:04:06.392
Captures demographics, photo, 10 fingerprints and 2 irises at an enrolment centre, signed and encrypted on the device.

72
00:04:06.672 --> 00:04:07.845
Enrolment server:

73
00:04:08.125 --> 00:04:15.187
Receives packets, runs quality checks and manual review, then de-duplication against everyone already enrolled.

74
00:04:15.467 --> 00:04:18.816
ABIS (automated biometric identification system):

75
00:04:19.096 --> 00:04:22.744
1:N biometric search across more than a billion records.

76
00:04:23.024 --> 00:04:24.283
ID generation:

77
00:04:24.563 --> 00:04:30.067
Issues a random, non-meaningful 12-digit number with a checksum after de-dup passes.

78
00:04:30.347 --> 00:04:31.968
Identity repository:

79
00:04:32.248 --> 00:04:34.723
The master record, with a history of updates.

80
00:04:36.073 --> 00:04:44.051
Authentication service: one-on-one match of a given ID plus biometric or OTP, answered yes or no. eKYC:

81
00:04:44.331 --> 00:04:49.259
With resident consent, returns the demographics and photo to a regulated entity.

82
00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:51.203
Virtual ID and tokens:

83
00:04:51.483 --> 00:04:57.521
Revocable alias IDs and per-agency tokens, so the real number need not be shared or stored.

84
00:04:57.801 --> 00:04:59.251
Ecosystem management:

85
00:04:59.531 --> 00:05:03.627
Registered agencies, device certification, licence keys, audit.

86
00:05:03.907 --> 00:05:05.294
Resident self-service:

87
00:05:05.574 --> 00:05:08.667
Update address, lock biometrics, see auth history.

88
00:05:10.017 --> 00:05:11.724
Vaccination registration.

89
00:05:13.074 --> 00:05:14.418
Here is a quick table:

90
00:05:14.698 --> 00:05:16.213
System, and What it does.

91
00:05:16.493 --> 00:05:17.623
Registration:

92
00:05:17.903 --> 00:05:24.602
Mobile OTP login, beneficiaries linked to a phone (up to N per phone), ID document details.

93
00:05:24.882 --> 00:05:26.631
Centre and session management:

94
00:05:26.911 --> 00:05:31.583
Centres, vaccines, daily sessions with capacity, age groups, dose numbers.

95
00:05:31.863 --> 00:05:33.314
Slot search and booking:

96
00:05:33.594 --> 00:05:37.050
Search by pincode or district, book a slot, reschedule.

97
00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.439
Vaccinator app:

98
00:05:38.719 --> 00:05:43.007
Verify the beneficiary at the centre, record the dose (works offline).

99
00:05:43.287 --> 00:05:44.589
Certificate service:

100
00:05:44.869 --> 00:05:48.367
Signed certificate with a QR code, available instantly.

101
00:05:48.647 --> 00:05:49.778
Verification:

102
00:05:50.058 --> 00:05:53.450
Anyone scans the QR and checks the signature offline.

103
00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.138
Supply and dashboards:

104
00:05:55.418 --> 00:06:00.239
Doses administered, coverage by region, wastage, adverse events (AEFI).

105
00:06:01.589 --> 00:06:02.763
Open APIs:

106
00:06:03.043 --> 00:06:06.797
Third-party apps can show availability and download certificates.

107
00:06:08.147 --> 00:06:13.289
Scale (assumptions for this design, with public orders of magnitude marked VERIFY).

108
00:06:13.569 --> 00:06:14.913
Here is a quick table:

109
00:06:15.193 --> 00:06:16.515
Quantity, and Value.

110
00:06:16.795 --> 00:06:22.854
Identities: ~1.3B+ (publicly reported order of magnitude, VERIFY).

111
00:06:23.134 --> 00:06:24.883
Biometric template gallery:

112
00:06:25.163 --> 00:06:29.302
1.3B times (10 fingers + 2 irises + face).

113
00:06:29.582 --> 00:06:31.054
Enrolments and updates:

114
00:06:31.334 --> 00:06:33.958
Hundreds of thousands to over a million a day.

115
00:06:34.238 --> 00:06:35.390
Authentication:

116
00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:42.518
Tens of crores (hundreds of millions) of transactions a month, with daily peaks over 100M (VERIFY).

117
00:06:42.798 --> 00:06:44.377
Vaccine registrations:

118
00:06:44.657 --> 00:06:48.411
Hundreds of millions within months; tens of millions on peak days.

119
00:06:48.691 --> 00:06:50.697
Slot-search traffic at a new release:

120
00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:53.857
Millions of requests per minute, heavy bot traffic.

121
00:06:54.137 --> 00:06:55.694
Vaccinations recorded:

122
00:06:55.974 --> 00:07:02.673
Peak days of tens of millions of doses (a peak above 20M a day was publicly reported, VERIFY).

123
00:07:04.023 --> 00:07:05.047
Certificates:

124
00:07:05.327 --> 00:07:08.292
Billions of doses, each with a signed certificate.

125
00:07:09.642 --> 00:07:11.029
Estimation highlights.

126
00:07:11.309 --> 00:07:12.354
De-dup cost:

127
00:07:12.634 --> 00:07:23.621
Comparing one new enrollee against 1.3B people with 10 fingers + 2 irises is ~1.3B times several comparisons each, per enrolment.

128
00:07:23.901 --> 00:07:29.149
At 1M enrolments a day, that is on the order of 10^15 comparisons a day.

129
00:07:29.429 --> 00:07:41.013
This is only feasible with fast template matchers (for example, iris codes compared with bit operations), indexing or binning to prune candidates, and a massive parallel cluster.

130
00:07:42.363 --> 00:07:48.613
Historically this was spread across multiple independent ABIS vendors (publicly described, VERIFY).

131
00:07:48.893 --> 00:07:50.045
Authentication:

132
00:07:50.325 --> 00:07:58.347
100M auths a day averages ~1,200 per second, with peaks of perhaps 5 to 10k per second.

133
00:07:58.627 --> 00:08:02.765
Each is a one-on-one match: fetch this person's template and compare.

134
00:08:03.045 --> 00:08:04.027
That is cheap.

135
00:08:04.307 --> 00:08:08.637
The hard parts are latency across the country, availability and security.

136
00:08:08.917 --> 00:08:10.368
Vaccine slot booking:

137
00:08:11.718 --> 00:08:17.777
A release of 1M slots with 50M people trying means slot demand far exceeds supply.

138
00:08:18.057 --> 00:08:20.617
The design goal is to be fair and not crash.

139
00:08:20.897 --> 00:08:22.859
Serving everyone is impossible.

140
00:08:24.209 --> 00:08:25.084
Part 2.

141
00:08:25.364 --> 00:08:26.324
Requirements.

142
00:08:27.574 --> 00:08:29.345
Functional: digital ID.

143
00:08:29.625 --> 00:08:40.867
Enrol a resident with demographic and biometric data, operator and verifier signatures, and supporting documents (or an introducer for people without documents, VERIFY).

144
00:08:41.147 --> 00:08:48.102
De-duplicate: never issue two IDs to one person, and flag suspected duplicates for manual adjudication.

145
00:08:48.382 --> 00:08:50.238
Generate a unique random ID.

146
00:08:50.518 --> 00:08:53.142
Deliver the ID letter and a digital copy.

147
00:08:53.422 --> 00:09:01.486
Authenticate: yes or no answers for an ID plus a biometric, OTP or demographic data, within a few seconds.

148
00:09:02.836 --> 00:09:09.236
eKYC: with consent (biometric or OTP), share limited attributes with regulated entities.

149
00:09:09.516 --> 00:09:16.151
Updates: address, mobile number, biometric refresh (for children, at mandatory ages, VERIFY).

150
00:09:16.431 --> 00:09:25.156
Privacy features: a virtual ID that can be regenerated;. biometric locking;. an authentication history visible to the resident;.

151
00:09:26.506 --> 00:09:32.671
tokenised references for agencies (UID tokens), so agencies do not store the ID number.

152
00:09:32.951 --> 00:09:45.623
Ecosystem: onboarding of authentication user agencies and KYC agencies;. certified capture devices (registered devices, which encrypt and sign on the device);. audits.

153
00:09:46.973 --> 00:09:49.192
Functional: vaccination platform.

154
00:09:49.472 --> 00:09:51.456
Register with mobile OTP.

155
00:09:51.736 --> 00:09:55.640
Add beneficiaries using an ID document number, age and name.

156
00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:01.083
Browse centres, see availability by age group, vaccine and dose, and book or cancel.

157
00:10:01.363 --> 00:10:04.477
Walk-in registration at centres (the assisted mode).

158
00:10:04.757 --> 00:10:11.072
Vaccinator records the dose with batch number and time, captures AEFI, and schedules the next dose due date.

159
00:10:11.352 --> 00:10:22.104
Instant certificate by SMS link and download: verifiable and tamper-evident, with an international-travel format (ICAO or EU-compatible variants, VERIFY).

160
00:10:23.454 --> 00:10:29.385
Open APIs (public read-only availability, protected booking and certificate APIs).

161
00:10:29.665 --> 00:10:35.723
Dashboards: doses by region, age and vaccine, stock and wastage, and centre utilisation.

162
00:10:37.073 --> 00:10:38.183
Non-functional.

163
00:10:38.463 --> 00:10:39.807
Here is a quick table:

164
00:10:40.087 --> 00:10:41.452
Attribute, and Target.

165
00:10:41.732 --> 00:10:42.799
Availability:

166
00:10:43.079 --> 00:10:51.207
99.9%+ for authentication (people wait at ration shops and bank counters), with graceful degradation.

167
00:10:51.487 --> 00:10:52.532
Auth latency:

168
00:10:52.812 --> 00:10:57.036
1 to 2 s end to end at the agency, even on rural networks.

169
00:10:57.316 --> 00:10:58.468
De-dup accuracy:

170
00:10:58.748 --> 00:11:04.764
Extremely low false match rate (no wrong merges) and low false non-match rate (duplicates caught).

171
00:11:05.044 --> 00:11:05.961
Inclusion:

172
00:11:06.241 --> 00:11:11.703
Fallbacks for people whose biometrics fail (iris, face, OTP, exception handling).

173
00:11:11.983 --> 00:11:12.900
Privacy:

174
00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:20.817
Data minimisation, purpose limitation, no central tracking of the purpose of authentication (VERIFY what is logged).

175
00:11:21.097 --> 00:11:22.100
Security:

176
00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:27.820
End-to-end encryption from device to server, HSMs, signed packets, zero trust.

177
00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:30.642
Fairness under scarcity:

178
00:11:30.922 --> 00:11:34.591
No advantage for bots, spread demand, transparent rules.

179
00:11:34.871 --> 00:11:36.045
Verifiability:

180
00:11:36.325 --> 00:11:39.695
Certificates verifiable offline by anyone, forever.

181
00:11:39.975 --> 00:11:40.850
Openness:

182
00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:47.551
Open standards and APIs so the ecosystem can build on top (digital public infrastructure).

183
00:11:48.901 --> 00:11:49.797
Part 3.

184
00:11:50.077 --> 00:11:51.592
High-Level Architecture.

185
00:11:52.842 --> 00:11:54.186
Here is the diagram.

186
00:11:58.036 --> 00:11:59.081
Key decisions.

187
00:11:59.361 --> 00:12:00.705
Here is a quick table:

188
00:12:00.985 --> 00:12:02.756
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

189
00:12:03.036 --> 00:12:04.039
ID number.

190
00:12:04.319 --> 00:12:05.151
Choice:

191
00:12:05.431 --> 00:12:07.692
Random, non-meaningful, with a checksum.

192
00:12:07.972 --> 00:12:08.655
Why:

193
00:12:08.935 --> 00:12:15.313
No information leaks from the number itself, and it never needs re-issuing (publicly described, VERIFY).

194
00:12:15.593 --> 00:12:16.660
Minimal data.

195
00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:17.772
Choice:

196
00:12:18.052 --> 00:12:22.020
Store only what is needed: demographics, photo, biometrics.

197
00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:24.967
No caste, religion or income (VERIFY).

198
00:12:25.247 --> 00:12:25.929
Why:

199
00:12:26.209 --> 00:12:28.087
Privacy, and less to protect.

200
00:12:28.367 --> 00:12:29.945
Authentication response.

201
00:12:30.225 --> 00:12:31.057
Choice:

202
00:12:31.337 --> 00:12:36.372
Yes or no only (plus eKYC with consent), never raw biometrics.

203
00:12:36.652 --> 00:12:37.335
Why:

204
00:12:37.615 --> 00:12:39.087
Minimises exposure.

205
00:12:39.367 --> 00:12:40.924
Biometric security.

206
00:12:41.204 --> 00:12:42.036
Choice:

207
00:12:42.316 --> 00:12:46.177
Encrypted and signed at the capture device (registered devices).

208
00:12:46.457 --> 00:12:47.140
Why:

209
00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:50.513
Stops replay and tampering between device and server.

210
00:12:51.863 --> 00:12:52.653
De-dup.

211
00:12:52.933 --> 00:12:53.765
Choice:

212
00:12:54.045 --> 00:12:57.735
Multiple independent ABIS vendors, with results combined.

213
00:12:58.015 --> 00:12:58.698
Why:

214
00:12:58.978 --> 00:13:02.967
Accuracy and no vendor lock-in (publicly described, VERIFY).

215
00:13:03.247 --> 00:13:04.847
Federated ecosystem.

216
00:13:05.127 --> 00:13:05.959
Choice:

217
00:13:06.239 --> 00:13:10.719
Agencies connect via licensed gateways (ASAs), not directly.

218
00:13:10.999 --> 00:13:11.682
Why:

219
00:13:11.962 --> 00:13:14.181
Controlled access and accountability.

220
00:13:14.461 --> 00:13:15.805
Vaccine slot search.

221
00:13:16.085 --> 00:13:16.917
Choice:

222
00:13:17.197 --> 00:13:20.738
Heavily cached, read-only, open APIs, rate-limited.

223
00:13:21.018 --> 00:13:21.701
Why:

224
00:13:21.981 --> 00:13:23.901
Absorbs flash crowds and bots.

225
00:13:24.181 --> 00:13:25.205
Certificates.

226
00:13:25.485 --> 00:13:26.317
Choice:

227
00:13:26.597 --> 00:13:31.183
Digitally signed QR (verifiable credential), verifiable offline.

228
00:13:31.463 --> 00:13:32.146
Why:

229
00:13:32.426 --> 00:13:34.111
Trust without calling the server.

230
00:13:35.461 --> 00:13:36.379
Part 4.

231
00:13:36.659 --> 00:13:37.661
How It Works:

232
00:13:37.941 --> 00:13:39.157
End-to-End Journeys.

233
00:13:40.407 --> 00:13:42.498
Journey 1: enrolling a resident.

234
00:13:42.778 --> 00:13:56.581
At an enrolment centre, run by a registrar's agency with a certified operator: the operator logs in with their own biometric;. the resident gives name, date of birth, gender, address, optional mobile and email,

235
00:13:56.861 --> 00:14:14.226
and supporting documents (or verification by an introducer or head of family where rules allow, VERIFY);. the kit captures a photo, 10 fingerprints and 2 irises, with quality checks at capture (retake a poor-quality finger; record exceptions for missing fingers or eyes).

236
00:14:15.576 --> 00:14:32.301
Packet creation: demographics, biometrics and document scans are bundled into an enrolment packet;. the packet is encrypted with the ID authority's public key and signed by the operator and supervisor;. it is stored on the kit and uploaded when connected,

237
00:14:32.581 --> 00:14:42.181
with an enrolment ID receipt handed to the resident;. the kit may be offline for days in remote areas, so packets carry timestamps and signatures.

238
00:14:43.531 --> 00:14:57.419
At central ingest: decrypt the packet in a secure zone;. validate the signatures, operator and device;. run quality and fraud checks (operator anomalies, duplicate packets);. run demographic de-dup as a quick filter.

239
00:14:57.699 --> 00:15:04.078
Biometric de-dup (1:N): templates (fingerprint minutiae, iris codes, face embeddings) are extracted;.

240
00:15:05.428 --> 00:15:14.281
they are searched against the entire gallery by ABIS, possibly more than one vendor, whose results are fused (§6.1 to 6.3).

241
00:15:14.561 --> 00:15:28.961
Outcomes: unique, then proceed;. clear duplicate, then reject, and tell the resident they already have an ID;. ambiguous (possible match with low confidence), then manual adjudication, where trained experts compare images.

242
00:15:29.241 --> 00:15:30.500
ID generation:

243
00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:50.943
generate a random 12-digit number, excluding certain patterns, with a Verhoeff checksum (publicly described, VERIFY);. write to the identity repository;. add templates to the ABIS galleries and the authentication template store;. send a letter by post and make an electronic download available.

244
00:15:51.223 --> 00:15:57.474
Timeline: days to weeks, since the heavy compute and manual adjudication are batch pipelines with queues.

245
00:15:58.824 --> 00:16:02.280
Journey 2: fingerprint authentication at a ration shop.

246
00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:08.107
The fair-price shop operator enters the resident's ID (or scans the QR code on the card).

247
00:16:08.387 --> 00:16:11.928
The resident places a finger on a registered device (RD).

248
00:16:12.208 --> 00:16:26.757
The device: captures the fingerprint;. checks liveness as supported;. builds the PID block (biometric data plus timestamp);. encrypts it with a session key, which is itself encrypted with the ID authority's public key;.

249
00:16:28.107 --> 00:16:37.878
signs it with the device's private key (certified device, with the key in secure hardware);. so the application software never sees raw biometrics.

250
00:16:38.158 --> 00:16:53.155
The shop's application (the AUA, for example, the food department's system) builds the auth request (ID or VID or token, PID block, AUA code, licence key, transaction ID) and signs it with the AUA's key.

251
00:16:53.435 --> 00:17:01.990
The request goes through an ASA, a licensed network gateway with secure leased lines, to the ID authority's authentication service.

252
00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:03.870
The authentication service:

253
00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:27.257
validates the AUA and ASA licences and signatures, the device certificate and the timestamp (preventing replay);. decrypts the PID in an HSM-backed secure environment;. looks up the templates for that ID (one-on-one);. matches them (fingerprint matcher, with thresholds tuned for one-on-one);. responds Yes or No with an error code, signed.

254
00:17:27.537 --> 00:17:31.335
There is no personal data unless eKYC was requested.

255
00:17:31.615 --> 00:17:32.383
Logging:

256
00:17:33.733 --> 00:17:46.298
the authority logs the transaction: which AUA, time, result, device, without the purpose of the transaction (publicly described, VERIFY);. the resident can see their authentication history.

257
00:17:46.578 --> 00:17:56.135
Failure paths: retry with another finger, then an iris or face, then OTP to the registered mobile;. or a manual exception with officer approval.

258
00:17:57.485 --> 00:18:04.845
Worn fingerprints (manual labourers, the elderly) are the classic inclusion issue, so the design must support fallbacks.

259
00:18:05.125 --> 00:18:09.989
Performance: a template lookup from a key-value store, plus a match in milliseconds.

260
00:18:10.269 --> 00:18:14.003
End-to-end latency is dominated by the network and device.

261
00:18:15.353 --> 00:18:18.382
Journey 3: eKYC to open a bank account.

262
00:18:18.662 --> 00:18:25.190
The bank (a KUA, a KYC user agency) asks the customer for consent for eKYC.

263
00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:29.033
The customer authenticates by biometric or OTP.

264
00:18:29.313 --> 00:18:32.705
The request includes an eKYC flag and consent.

265
00:18:32.985 --> 00:18:51.801
On success, the authority returns signed and encrypted demographics and photo to the bank: the bank verifies the signature;. the signed record is equivalent to a verified document, if permitted by regulation (VERIFY RBI or PMLA rules on Aadhaar eKYC eligibility).

266
00:18:53.151 --> 00:19:12.329
Privacy features: the bank may receive a token instead of storing the ID number;. reference keys go into a vault with restricted access;. offline KYC options (a signed XML or QR the resident downloads and shares, without an online auth, VERIFY) avoid central logging altogether.

267
00:19:13.679 --> 00:19:17.754
Journey 4: vaccine registration and the 4 pm flash crowd.

268
00:19:18.034 --> 00:19:38.983
Registration (days before): enter a mobile number and receive an OTP (via an SMS gateway, with rate limits);. add up to N beneficiaries with an ID proof type and number, name, birth year and gender;. where an ID is used, optional demographic auth or de-dup on (ID type, number) ensures one registration per person.

269
00:19:39.263 --> 00:19:43.573
Release announced: slots for 18 to 44 open at 4 pm.

270
00:19:43.853 --> 00:19:47.906
Millions wait with the page open, and bot scripts poll the APIs.

271
00:19:49.256 --> 00:20:11.955
At 4 pm, slot search: search by district or pincode for a date range;. responses are cached per district and date with short TTLs (tens of seconds) at the CDN and in service caches;. the public API is rate-limited per IP and the available counts are approximate;. the protected booking API needs an OTP login token.

272
00:20:12.235 --> 00:20:12.981
Booking:

273
00:20:14.331 --> 00:20:31.206
the user selects a session and slot and passes a CAPTCHA (bot defence);. the booking service atomically decrements capacity for (session, dose) using a conditional update: if available ≥ 1, decrement and create the appointment (Ch 48).

274
00:20:31.486 --> 00:20:35.582
If a slot is gone, the user gets fully booked and the search refreshes.

275
00:20:35.862 --> 00:20:41.771
Rules are enforced: one active appointment per beneficiary, correct age group, dose interval.

276
00:20:43.121 --> 00:20:48.561
Confirmation: an appointment slip goes out by SMS, and the centre's list is updated.

277
00:20:48.841 --> 00:21:01.684
What goes wrong without design: the database melts from search traffic;. bots grab all slots in seconds;. users see stale available counts and fail repeatedly;. the OTP service is overwhelmed.

278
00:21:01.964 --> 00:21:06.657
Mitigations are covered in §5.6 and §6.6.

279
00:21:08.007 --> 00:21:11.378
Journey 5: vaccination at the centre and the certificate.

280
00:21:11.658 --> 00:21:14.133
Verification: the beneficiary arrives.

281
00:21:14.413 --> 00:21:25.207
The vaccinator app (on a tablet or phone) searches the appointment by reference or mobile number and verifies the ID proof photo, or registers a walk-in on the spot.

282
00:21:25.487 --> 00:21:34.810
Record the dose: vaccine name, batch number, dose number, vaccinator ID, centre and time;. the next dose due date is computed automatically.

283
00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:39.591
If offline, the app stores records locally with signatures and syncs later.

284
00:21:39.871 --> 00:21:42.623
The certificate is then generated after sync.

285
00:21:43.973 --> 00:22:04.731
Certificate: the certificate service creates a record: beneficiary name, birth year or age, gender, ID reference (masked), vaccine, dose, date, centre and certificate ID;. it digitally signs it (§6.7) with the programme's private key kept in an HSM;. it encodes the signed payload in a QR code,

286
00:22:05.011 --> 00:22:15.016
rendered as a PDF;. the beneficiary receives an SMS with a download link, also available via partner apps and digital document lockers (VERIFY).

287
00:22:16.366 --> 00:22:29.038
Verification anywhere: a verifier app, at an airport, office or hotel, scans the QR code;. it checks the signature with the published public key, offline;. it displays the details.

288
00:22:29.318 --> 00:22:31.430
Tampering invalidates the signature.

289
00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:41.310
This is a verifiable credential pattern (W3C VC-like; publicly described as based on an open-source credentialing platform, VERIFY).

290
00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:47.716
Post-vaccination: adverse events (AEFI) are reported and flow to surveillance.

291
00:22:47.996 --> 00:22:50.577
Second-dose reminders go out on the due date.

292
00:22:51.927 --> 00:22:54.423
Journey 6: resident privacy controls.

293
00:22:54.703 --> 00:22:57.925
The resident logs in to self-service with an OTP.

294
00:22:58.205 --> 00:23:06.695
Biometric lock: the resident locks biometric authentication, so any biometric auth fails until they unlock it temporarily.

295
00:23:06.975 --> 00:23:09.087
This guards against fingerprint spoofing.

296
00:23:09.367 --> 00:23:18.541
Virtual ID: the resident generates a VID, a random 16-digit alias mapped to the ID, which can be revoked and regenerated.

297
00:23:18.821 --> 00:23:22.191
Agencies accept the VID instead of the ID number.

298
00:23:22.471 --> 00:23:28.701
Auth history: the resident sees which agency authenticated them and when, which helps detect misuse.

299
00:23:30.051 --> 00:23:31.011
Part 5.

300
00:23:31.291 --> 00:23:32.827
Core Subsystems in Depth.

301
00:23:34.077 --> 00:23:35.378
Enrolment pipeline.

302
00:23:35.658 --> 00:23:46.986
Stages: ingest, then decrypt, then validate, then quality, then demographic de-dup, then biometric de-dup, then adjudication, then ID generation, then print or dispatch.

303
00:23:47.266 --> 00:23:53.410
Each stage is a queue-driven worker pool with idempotency by enrolment ID, so it is re-runnable.

304
00:23:53.690 --> 00:24:00.026
Backpressure: the ABIS has finite capacity, so the ingest queue absorbs bursts (enrolment drives).

305
00:24:00.306 --> 00:24:11.847
Operator fraud detection: patterns such as many enrolments with the same biometrics under different names, or many exceptions (missing fingers) from one operator, lead to suspension.

306
00:24:13.197 --> 00:24:18.317
Rejections and reasons are sent back to the resident via the enrolment ID status.

307
00:24:19.667 --> 00:24:20.435
ABIS:

308
00:24:20.715 --> 00:24:22.955
1:N search at population scale.

309
00:24:23.235 --> 00:24:27.289
Gallery partitioning: billions of templates are split across many nodes.

310
00:24:27.569 --> 00:24:30.257
Each node holds a shard in memory for speed.

311
00:24:30.537 --> 00:24:35.166
Search: a query template is broadcast to all shards, which compare in parallel.

312
00:24:35.446 --> 00:24:38.518
The top-k candidates by score go to a combiner.

313
00:24:38.798 --> 00:24:47.715
Multi-modal fusion: fingerprint, iris and face scores are combined (score-level fusion) for final decisions (§6.3).

314
00:24:47.995 --> 00:24:52.049
Multi-vendor: gallery subsets or full copies across vendors.

315
00:24:52.329 --> 00:24:59.881
Decisions compare or combine results, and disagreements go to manual adjudication (publicly described, VERIFY).

316
00:25:01.231 --> 00:25:12.495
Throughput tuning: binning or indexing narrows candidates (for example, fingerprint pattern classes, iris code prefilters), and hardware (SIMD or GPU) speeds up matching.

317
00:25:13.845 --> 00:25:15.317
Authentication service.

318
00:25:15.597 --> 00:25:22.850
Stateless front ends validate signatures, licences and replay (timestamp window plus transaction ID de-dup).

319
00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:28.143
The template store is a key-value lookup from the ID (or its token) to the templates.

320
00:25:28.423 --> 00:25:32.775
It is replicated across data centres, and templates are encrypted at rest.

321
00:25:33.055 --> 00:25:38.175
Matching engines run a one-on-one match per modality with configurable thresholds.

322
00:25:38.455 --> 00:25:47.437
Active-active data centres: the authority publicly describes multiple DCs (VERIFY), and ASAs route to the nearest healthy DC.

323
00:25:48.787 --> 00:25:51.219
Rate limits and quotas apply per AUA.

324
00:25:51.499 --> 00:25:56.192
Anomaly detection covers the pattern of failures per device or agency.

325
00:25:56.472 --> 00:26:02.381
The response is signed (so an AUA can prove the result) and includes a response code for audit.

326
00:26:03.731 --> 00:26:05.630
Token vault and virtual IDs.

327
00:26:05.910 --> 00:26:09.665
VID: a random 16-digit alias that maps to the ID.

328
00:26:09.945 --> 00:26:12.803
A resident can have one active VID at a time.

329
00:26:13.083 --> 00:26:14.598
A new one revokes the old.

330
00:26:14.878 --> 00:26:24.286
UID tokens: a per-AUA or KUA token derived from the ID, so different agencies cannot link records by a common identifier.

331
00:26:24.566 --> 00:26:35.915
Reference key vault at agencies: they must store ID numbers only in an HSM-backed vault, referenced by a random reference key elsewhere in their systems (VERIFY the regulation).

332
00:26:37.265 --> 00:26:39.249
Registered device ecosystem.

333
00:26:39.529 --> 00:26:46.463
Certification: device models are certified, and each device has a key pair provisioned in secure hardware.

334
00:26:46.743 --> 00:26:51.969
Its certificate is signed by the manufacturer's CA, which is trusted by the authority.

335
00:26:52.249 --> 00:26:57.220
Management server: device keys rotate, and compromised devices are revoked.

336
00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:00.721
Result: biometrics are encrypted inside the device.

337
00:27:01.001 --> 00:27:04.863
Host applications never see or replay raw biometrics.

338
00:27:06.213 --> 00:27:08.303
Vaccine platform under flash crowds.

339
00:27:08.583 --> 00:27:24.839
Separate read and write paths: search is served from pre-computed availability snapshots per district and date, refreshed every few seconds from the bookings DB;. snapshots are pushed to a cache and the CDN;. booking hits the primary with atomic operations.

340
00:27:25.119 --> 00:27:29.983
Protect the core: rate limits at the edge (IP, token);. a CAPTCHA on booking;.

341
00:27:31.333 --> 00:27:32.635
OTP throttles;.

342
00:27:32.915 --> 00:27:37.288
WAF bot signatures;. a cap on booking attempts per user per minute.

343
00:27:37.568 --> 00:27:44.011
Spread demand: publish release schedules by state;. release in batches;. allow centre-specific timings;.

344
00:27:45.361 --> 00:27:55.771
notification-based access (a queue or waiting room at release time, with random admission among those present at T0, which is fairer than fastest click wins).

345
00:27:56.051 --> 00:28:00.403
Consistency: the capacity counter in the DB is the source of truth.

346
00:28:00.683 --> 00:28:05.995
The cache may say available while the booking fails, so the UI explains this and refreshes.

347
00:28:06.275 --> 00:28:11.801
Open APIs: the public read API lets ecosystem apps show availability.

348
00:28:12.081 --> 00:28:18.374
It is rate-limited, and some data is delayed to reduce bot advantage (VERIFY actual policies).

349
00:28:19.724 --> 00:28:21.985
Certificate issuance and verification.

350
00:28:22.265 --> 00:28:27.897
Signing service: stateless workers call an HSM with keys rotated per policy.

351
00:28:28.177 --> 00:28:32.871
Signatures are compact (ECDSA or EdDSA), so they fit in a QR code.

352
00:28:33.151 --> 00:28:36.649
Key distribution: public keys are published with key IDs.

353
00:28:36.929 --> 00:28:39.937
Verifier apps embed them and update periodically.

354
00:28:40.217 --> 00:28:42.991
Revocation: rare, for fraudulent records.

355
00:28:43.271 --> 00:28:46.172
The verifier can check a revocation list when online.

356
00:28:46.452 --> 00:28:55.049
Formats: national format plus interoperable formats for travel (for example, EU DCC-compatible or ICAO, VERIFY).

357
00:28:56.399 --> 00:29:01.007
Corrections: for errors in name or date, re-issue with a new certificate ID.

358
00:29:01.287 --> 00:29:03.741
The old certificate is marked superseded.

359
00:29:05.091 --> 00:29:06.029
Part 6.

360
00:29:06.309 --> 00:29:07.269
Algorithms.

361
00:29:08.519 --> 00:29:09.757
Fingerprint matching.

362
00:29:10.037 --> 00:29:15.647
Features: minutiae, meaning ridge endings and bifurcations, each with position and angle.

363
00:29:15.927 --> 00:29:19.405
A good fingerprint has ~40 to 100 minutiae.

364
00:29:19.685 --> 00:29:29.839
Matching: align two minutiae sets (rotation, translation, distortion);. count the corresponding minutiae within tolerance;. compute a similarity score.

365
00:29:30.119 --> 00:29:36.071
Algorithms use local structures (minutia triplets, cylinder codes) for alignment-free matching.

366
00:29:37.421 --> 00:29:38.339
Standards:

367
00:29:38.619 --> 00:29:47.003
ISO or IEC 19794 to 2 minutiae templates enable interoperability across vendors.

368
00:29:47.283 --> 00:29:48.093
Quality:

369
00:29:48.373 --> 00:29:53.920
NFIQ-style quality scores at capture drive retakes, since poor quality means higher error rates.

370
00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:56.827
Iris and face matching.

371
00:29:57.107 --> 00:30:17.502
Iris (Daugman's method): segment the iris;. normalise it to a rectangle (rubber-sheet model);. apply Gabor filters;. produce a ~2,048-bit iris code with a mask;. compare two codes by fractional Hamming distance, using XOR, AND masks and popcount;. a small distance means the same eye.

372
00:30:18.852 --> 00:30:26.745
This is extremely fast (bit operations) and very discriminating, which makes it ideal for 1:N de-dup at a billion scale.

373
00:30:27.025 --> 00:30:33.532
Face: deep CNN embeddings (for example, 512-dim) compared by cosine similarity.

374
00:30:33.812 --> 00:30:38.569
These are used for one-on-one face auth and as an additional modality in fusion.

375
00:30:38.849 --> 00:30:43.159
Accuracy varies with lighting, age and pose, so thresholds need care.

376
00:30:44.509 --> 00:30:46.599
De-duplication decision and fusion.

377
00:30:46.879 --> 00:30:49.781
Per candidate, compute scores for each modality:

378
00:30:50.061 --> 00:30:52.109
10 fingers, 2 irises, face.

379
00:30:52.389 --> 00:31:03.141
Fusion: normalise the scores (z-score or likelihood ratios);. combine them (sum, or a weighted or logistic model trained on known genuine and impostor pairs).

380
00:31:03.421 --> 00:31:08.733
Thresholds: above the high threshold, then duplicate;. below the low threshold, then unique;.

381
00:31:10.083 --> 00:31:12.493
in between, then manual adjudication.

382
00:31:12.773 --> 00:31:33.851
Error rates: the False Match Rate (FMR) and False Non-Match Rate (FNMR) trade off;. at 1:N with N = 1B, even an FMR of 10^minus 10 per comparison gives ~0.1 false candidates per search, so thresholds must be extremely strict, with multi-modal fusion to keep FNMR low.

383
00:31:34.131 --> 00:31:38.077
Public reports have cited very low de-dup error rates (VERIFY).

384
00:31:39.427 --> 00:31:54.510
Special cases: twins (iris differs even for identical twins, while face may not), children (biometrics change, so updates are needed), and people with missing biometrics (use the available modalities with stricter manual checks).

385
00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:58.953
Liveness detection (presentation attack detection).

386
00:31:59.233 --> 00:32:06.572
Attacks: silicone or gelatin fake fingers, printed irises, photos or videos of faces, masks, deepfakes.

387
00:32:06.852 --> 00:32:25.348
Defences: hardware (multispectral sensors, pulse detection, temperature);. software (texture analysis, distortion patterns for fingers; challenge-response for face such as blink, head turn, or passive CNN-based liveness);. device certification requiring a PAD level (VERIFY);.

388
00:32:26.698 --> 00:32:28.959
plus a biometric lock for residents.

389
00:32:30.309 --> 00:32:31.653
ID number design.

390
00:32:31.933 --> 00:32:43.560
Random generation: sample uniformly from the valid space, excluding patterns (for example, not starting with 0 or 1, VERIFY), and check for no collision with issued IDs.

391
00:32:43.840 --> 00:32:50.944
Verhoeff checksum: detects all single-digit errors and all adjacent transpositions, the typical typing errors.

392
00:32:51.224 --> 00:32:53.464
It is better than Luhn for transpositions.

393
00:32:53.744 --> 00:33:02.725
Why random: no information leakage (no birth date or region encoded), no re-issue when details change, and hard to guess sequential IDs.

394
00:33:04.075 --> 00:33:06.251
Fair slot allocation under scarcity.

395
00:33:06.531 --> 00:33:13.337
Naive approach: first come, first served at 4 pm, which rewards bots and people with fast internet.

396
00:33:13.617 --> 00:33:14.790
Better approaches:

397
00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:23.923
Randomised lottery among those present: everyone who registers interest in a window gets a random position in a queue, then books in order.

398
00:33:24.203 --> 00:33:26.337
This is fair across connection speeds.

399
00:33:26.617 --> 00:33:34.019
Rate-limited booking tokens: issue a booking token per user per window, and each booking needs a token plus a CAPTCHA.

400
00:33:35.369 --> 00:33:39.871
Staggered releases by region or age, with published schedules to spread load.

401
00:33:40.151 --> 00:33:46.615
Priority rules set by policy: health workers first, then the elderly, those with comorbidities, and so on.

402
00:33:46.895 --> 00:33:52.740
Reservation holds: hold a slot briefly (for example, 2 minutes) while the user confirms.

403
00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:54.428
Release it on timeout.

404
00:33:54.708 --> 00:33:57.801
Capacity counters: an atomic decrement per session.

405
00:33:58.081 --> 00:34:04.609
For very hot sessions, partition capacity into buckets (as in inventory sharding in I02).

406
00:34:05.959 --> 00:34:07.879
Verifiable credential signing.

407
00:34:08.159 --> 00:34:12.469
Payload: canonical JSON (or CBOR) of the certificate fields.

408
00:34:12.749 --> 00:34:13.666
Signature:

409
00:34:13.946 --> 00:34:22.821
ECDSA P-256 or Ed25519 over the canonical bytes, then embedded with a key ID.

410
00:34:23.101 --> 00:34:29.458
Encoding: compress (for example, zlib), then base45 or base64, then the QR code.

411
00:34:29.738 --> 00:34:41.557
The EU DCC uses COSE, CBOR and base45 (publicly documented; the Indian format was publicly described as a signed VC in JSON-LD, VERIFY).

412
00:34:41.837 --> 00:34:48.173
Verification: the verifier recomputes the canonical form and checks the signature with the public key.

413
00:34:48.453 --> 00:34:49.903
No network is needed.

414
00:34:51.253 --> 00:35:02.944
Selective disclosure (advanced): future credentials may let the holder reveal only vaccinated: yes and not their name or age (BBS+ signatures, SD-JWT).

415
00:35:04.294 --> 00:35:05.937
Bot and abuse detection.

416
00:35:06.217 --> 00:35:18.057
Signals: request rates per IP, token and device;. automation fingerprints (headless browsers, missing JS signals);. timing regularity;. many beneficiaries from one account;.

417
00:35:18.337 --> 00:35:19.979
OTP request bursts.

418
00:35:20.259 --> 00:35:28.793
Response: step-up challenges (CAPTCHA), throttling, token revocation and temporary blocks, with monitoring for false positives.

419
00:35:30.143 --> 00:35:31.081
Part 7.

420
00:35:31.361 --> 00:35:32.684
Data Architecture.

421
00:35:33.934 --> 00:35:35.321
Operational stores.

422
00:35:35.601 --> 00:35:36.945
Here is a quick table:

423
00:35:37.225 --> 00:35:39.401
Data, and Store (reference), and Notes.

424
00:35:39.681 --> 00:35:40.897
Enrolment packets.

425
00:35:41.177 --> 00:35:42.414
Store (reference):

426
00:35:42.694 --> 00:35:45.723
Encrypted object storage, retained per policy.

427
00:35:46.003 --> 00:35:46.814
Notes:

428
00:35:47.094 --> 00:35:47.969
Evidence.

429
00:35:48.249 --> 00:35:49.913
Identity repository.

430
00:35:50.193 --> 00:35:51.430
Store (reference):

431
00:35:51.710 --> 00:35:56.809
Partitioned RDBMS or NoSQL with strict access, versioned records.

432
00:35:57.089 --> 00:35:57.899
Notes:

433
00:35:58.179 --> 00:35:59.246
Master data.

434
00:35:59.526 --> 00:36:00.955
Biometric templates.

435
00:36:01.235 --> 00:36:02.473
Store (reference):

436
00:36:02.753 --> 00:36:07.467
In-memory ABIS galleries plus an encrypted template KV store for one-on-one.

437
00:36:07.747 --> 00:36:08.558
Notes:

438
00:36:08.838 --> 00:36:10.502
Separated from demographics.

439
00:36:10.782 --> 00:36:12.467
